---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import portrait from '../assets/portrait.jpg';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import { research } from '../data/research';
import { work } from '../data/work';

// Split the thesis so exactly one word carries the accent.
const [before, after] = site.thesis.split(site.thesisAccent);
---

<Base title={`${site.name} · ${site.role}`}>
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">{site.role}</p>
    <h1 class="thesis">{before}<span class="accent">{site.thesisAccent}</span>{after}</h1>
  </section>

  <section id="about" class="wrap section">
    <div class="about-grid">
      <div>
        <h2 class="label">About</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            A model produces an answer; a person has to decide whether to trust it. My
            research proposed one way in: instead of asking which features mattered, ask
            which training examples were <span class="accent">responsible</span>. Inspect
            the process, not the output. It became a thesis and two papers.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then I went to industry. Now the work is putting AI in the hands of energy
            engineers, people who have no use for a confident wrong answer.
          </p>
          <p>
            Which makes it production work more than modelling work. An inference service
            other products depend on. Model choice and provider routing behind a versioned
            interface, so what's underneath can change without anyone downstream noticing.
            Retrieval that returns the right passage rather than a plausible one. Output
            validated before a human ever sees it. Evaluation offline, quality monitoring
            online, latency and cost tracked like any other budget. Prompts in version
            control, because they're code.
          </p>
          <p>
            All of it in a startup, where you own a product rather than a ticket. I own the
            platform and the conversational assistant inside it, from the argument about
            what to build, through architecture and code review, to whatever happens after
            it ships.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of that is glamorous. It's the difference between a model that works in a
            notebook and one someone trusts at three in the morning.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="portrait">
        <Image src={portrait} alt="Faraz Khadivpour" widths={[320, 640]} sizes="320px" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="research" class="wrap section">
    <h2 class="label">Research</h2>
    <div class="rows">
      {
        research.map((paper) => {
          const Tag = paper.href ? 'a' : 'div';
          return (
            <Tag class="row" {...(paper.href ? { href: paper.href, rel: 'noopener' } : {})}>
              <span class="body">
                <span class="title">
                  {paper.title}
                  {paper.note && <span style="color:var(--text-muted)"> ({paper.note})</span>}
                </span>
              </span>
              <span class="meta">
                {paper.venue} · {paper.year}
              </span>
            </Tag>
          );
        })
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="work" class="wrap section">
    <h2 class="label">Work</h2>
    <div class="rows">
      {
        work.map((item, i) => (
          <div class="row entry">
            <span class="num">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <span class="body">
              <span class="title">{item.title}</span>
              <p class="sub">{item.body}</p>
            </span>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
